==========================================================================================================================
PART B -- THE CRUX: is a BARYON-FRACTION kernel outside L6's closed class, or does it collapse into it?
==========================================================================================================================
  The mechanism, stated precisely.  The deposited action's MOND term is J(Y) with Y = |grad phi|^2 and a coupling 2(2
  - K_B) J^mu d_mu phi.  The proposal is to make the coupling -- equivalently the kernel's overall strength -- a
  function of the LOCAL BARYON FRACTION f_b = rho_b/(rho_b + rho_c), so that the MOND term is at full strength where
  baryons dominate and switched off where the cold component does:
        g = g_bar + f g_halo + s(f_b) a_0 Delta( g_src / a_0 ) ,      s(1) = 1,   s(f_b -> 0) -> 0.
  L6 closed S(X) for X a SINGLE environmental scalar: the potential, the baryon density, the acceleration, or the
  enclosed mass.  f_b is a RATIO OF TWO SPECIES.  L6's own closing note records that 'a non-monotone S, or S of two
  variables at once, is a fitted function rather than a screening mechanism.  Neither is claimed closed here.'  So the
  class is formally untested; B1-B3 decide whether it is genuinely new or collapses in practice.

      f_b at the CMB's own cold abundance f = 1:
        SPARC, enclosed-mass reading  : median 0.335, 10-90% [0.146, 0.789]
        SPARC, local-midplane reading : median 0.863, 10-90% [0.441, 0.947]   (h_z = 300 pc)
        clusters, enclosed            : median 0.112; local: 0.107
        the homogeneous background    : f_b = Omega_b/Omega_m = 0.1556

    B1  does f_b collapse into a function of one of L6's variables?  At the SAME value of X, do clusters
        and galaxies sit at the same f_b?  (If they do, s(f_b) IS an S(X) in disguise.)
          X = gb    overlap 100%   cluster-vs-galaxy separation in f_b: |z| =   38.6
          X = phi   overlap  47%   cluster-vs-galaxy separation in f_b: |z| =   59.7
          X = rho   overlap 100%   cluster-vs-galaxy separation in f_b: |z| =   15.0
          X = M     overlap  11%   cluster-vs-galaxy separation in f_b: |z| =    1.6
  [FAIL] B1 f_b COLLAPSES into a single-valued function of one of L6's environmental variables, i.e. the mechanism is L6's screening under a new name   (it does not. At the same acceleration, potential, baryon density or enclosed baryon mass the two populations sit at DIFFERENT baryon fractions, separated by |z| up to 60. f_b is not a function of any single L6 variable, because it carries information about a SECOND species that none of L6's variables contains. This is a genuinely new function, as L6's own closing note anticipated)

    B2  the cosmological ordering -- the horn that closed L6 without needing overlap.
          L6's variables : the background is BEYOND cluster outskirts in ALL of them (rho_b 1057x lower; Phi, g_bar -> 0).
          f_b            : cluster outskirts f_b = 0.1486 (enclosed) / 0.1916 (local); the background is f_b = 0.1556.
                           ratio background/outskirts = 1.05 -- the background is NOT beyond cluster outskirts, it sits essentially AT them.
        This is L7's measured fact read as a statement about the screening variable: clusters retain essentially the
        cosmic baryon fraction (f_bar = 0.149 against 0.156), so the sequence galaxy-disc -> cluster-outskirts ->
        background is NOT monotone in f_b; it TERMINATES at the cluster value. A monotone s(f_b) therefore assigns the
        background the same suppression as cluster outskirts, and generates no runaway.
  [FAIL] B2 the cosmological-ordering horn that closed L6 also fires on f_b, i.e. the background lies beyond cluster outskirts in the baryon fraction too   (it does not fire. In every L6 variable the background is orders of magnitude beyond cluster outskirts; in f_b it is a factor 1.05 away -- indistinguishable. The ordering argument needs the background to be MORE unscreened than clusters; in f_b it is EQUALLY screened, so the chain from cluster to cosmos is broken. This is L7's f_bar = 0.149 vs 0.156 read as a statement about the screening variable)

    B3  the SIGN, and whether the BBN horn can bite at all.
          L6's candidates were ENHANCEMENTS (E > 1) of a force whose unscreened value IS G_cosmo; the
          kill was G_cosmo/G_local >= 1.82 against BBN's 1.2. Here s <= 1 by
          construction: it is a SUPPRESSION, so the ratio moves the OTHER WAY.
          And the kernel is an ADDITIVE a_0-scale term, not a rescaling of G: at any BBN-era
          acceleration the fractional kernel contribution is a_0 Delta(g/a_0)/g <= 6.1e-05, so suppressing it changes G_eff by that much.
  [FAIL] B3 the BBN horn that closed L6 can bite on this mechanism, i.e. suppressing the kernel moves G_cosmo/G_local outside |G/G_0 - 1| < 0.2   (it cannot. s <= 1 is a suppression, the opposite sign to L6's enhancement; and the kernel is an additive a_0-scale term whose fractional size at BBN-era accelerations is 6.1e-05, so switching it off entirely changes G_eff by 1 part in 2e+04. BBN is blind to it)
  [FAIL] B4 [THE CRUX] a baryon-fraction-dependent kernel is INSIDE L6's closed class, so the lane is over before it starts   (it is NOT inside it, on three independent grounds: (i) f_b is not a single-valued function of any of L6's four variables (B1, |z| up to 60); (ii) the ordering horn does not fire because the background sits AT cluster outskirts in f_b rather than beyond them (B2, factor 1.05); (iii) the sign is a suppression, not an enhancement, and the additive a_0-scale kernel is invisible to BBN (B3). L6's own closing note already recorded that a function of two variables was untested. The mechanism therefore has to be tested on its own, which is Parts C-E -- and being outside a closed class is NOT evidence that it works)

==========================================================================================================================
PART C -- THE FUNCTION.  Can ONE s(f_b) be full strength in discs and suppressed at high cold fraction?
==========================================================================================================================
  This is where the brief says the mechanism is most likely to die, so it is checked directly and before any window is
  recomputed.  At the cold abundance the CMB fixes, galaxy discs are baryon-dominated in the inner regions and cold-
  dominated in the outer rotation curve -- which is exactly where MOND is needed.  Two readings of f_b are carried,
  because they are the two horns of the mechanism and they answer this question oppositely.

    C1  WHERE MOND IS NEEDED.  2059 of 2786 SPARC points (74%) have g_bar < a_0 -- the regime the kernel exists to explain.
        At f = 1 those points sit at f_b = 0.255 (enclosed reading; 10-90% [0.130, 0.517])
                                      f_b = 0.800 (local midplane; 10-90% [0.325, 0.922])
  [FAIL] C1 [THE MOST LIKELY WAY THIS DIES] at the cold abundance the CMB fixes, the points where MOND is needed are still BARYON-DOMINATED, so a suppression keyed to f_b leaves them alone   (on the reading that actually sets the rotation curve -- the ENCLOSED-mass fraction -- they are not: the median deep-MOND SPARC point sits at f_b = 0.255, i.e. COLD-DOMINATED, and 89% of them are below one half. The suppression switches on exactly where the kernel is supposed to work. (On the LOCAL midplane reading the answer is the opposite, f_b = 0.800; that is the second horn, C3-C4))

    C2  L6's own overlap machinery, run in the NEW variable (this is the fair test of the new class):
          f_b enclosed : overlap 100% of cluster rows, cluster-vs-galaxy required enhancement differs at |z| = 20.7
          f_b local    : overlap 100% of cluster rows, |z| = 9.8
  [FAIL] C2 in the new variable the two populations at the same f_b require the SAME kernel strength, so a single-valued s(f_b) exists on the data   (at the same enclosed baryon fraction clusters and galaxies require kernel strengths differing at |z| = 20.7 over 100% overlap -- L6's overlap horn fires in the new variable too, for the SAME reason it fired in the old ones: the required enhancement is not a function of the environment. (Local reading: |z| = 9.8 at 100% overlap))

        the exhibited functions.  Two one- and two-parameter families, both monotone, both s(1) = 1:
          s_pow(p)    = f_b^p                       (gentle; one parameter)
          s_sig(f*,n) = 1/(1 + (f*/f_b)^n)          (sharp; two parameters, n = sharpness)
          s = f_b^1       median s at deep-MOND SPARC points: 0.255 (enclosed) / 0.800 (local);  at cluster outskirts: 0.112
          s = f_b^3       median s at deep-MOND SPARC points: 0.017 (enclosed) / 0.512 (local);  at cluster outskirts: 0.001
          s: f*=0.5,n=4   median s at deep-MOND SPARC points: 0.063 (enclosed) / 0.868 (local);  at cluster outskirts: 0.003
          s: f*=0.2,n=8   median s at deep-MOND SPARC points: 0.874 (enclosed) / 1.000 (local);  at cluster outskirts: 0.010

    C3  is there a THRESHOLD in f_b separating the deep-MOND galaxy points from cluster rows?
          deep-MOND SPARC (enclosed): [0.025, 0.670], median 0.255
          cluster rows    (enclosed): [0.030, 0.289], median 0.112
          58% of the deep-MOND galaxy points lie INSIDE the cluster range
  [FAIL] C3 ONE monotone function of f_b can be full strength where MOND is needed in galaxies AND suppressed at cluster outskirts -- i.e. the two requirements are separated by a threshold in f_b   (they are not separated on the enclosed reading: 58% of the deep-MOND galaxy points fall inside the cluster f_b range, so any monotone s that switches OFF at cluster outskirts switches off at those galaxy points too. The transition cannot be made sharp enough because there is no gap to be sharp about)

    C4  the LOCAL horn, and its sensitivity.  A thin disc is locally baryon-dominated even where the
        ENCLOSED mass is not, so a kernel keyed to the local density ratio would survive in discs.
        Scale height h_z varied over the observed range for stellar+gas discs:
          h_z = 0.15 kpc : median f_b at deep-MOND SPARC points = 0.889, fraction below 0.5 =  10%
          h_z = 0.30 kpc : median f_b at deep-MOND SPARC points = 0.800, fraction below 0.5 =  16%
          h_z = 0.60 kpc : median f_b at deep-MOND SPARC points = 0.667, fraction below 0.5 =  29%
          h_z = 1.00 kpc : median f_b at deep-MOND SPARC points = 0.545, fraction below 0.5 =  43%
  [PASS] C4 the LOCAL-density reading rescues the disc, i.e. deep-MOND galaxy points stay baryon-dominated locally across the observed range of disc scale heights   (it does across most of the range -- median local f_b at deep-MOND points is 0.89 at h_z = 0.15 kpc, 0.80 at h_z = 0.30 kpc, 0.67 at h_z = 0.60 kpc, 0.55 at h_z = 1.00 kpc. That is the SECOND HORN and it is a genuine escape from C1/C3: on this reading the kernel is NOT switched off where MOND is needed, and Part D shows it is the horn that both opens the pincer and keeps part of the phenomenology. Its cost is that the one number it must hit is set by h_z (E2))

